Am 18.08.2011 20:09, schrieb Ed Kolis:
> Why is it necessary to manually #enhance one's weapon skills? Why
> can't the skills automatically level up?

You have a limited amount of slots and may prefer to use the slots
on something better than the one that serves you in the early game.

> Also, is it just me or does the whole weapon-skill system just serve
> the purpose of homogenizing weapon use for each character class?

 From what you wrote I don't see why you got that impression, and
what criticism you actually have, and in which way you would expect
a weapon proficiency system to be.

> That is, if I start as a Barbarian with a battle-axe, there is no
> incentive for me to swap to some other weapon (e.g. two-handed

There is an incentive for the classes to switch weapons; artifacts
are often better than what you start with, even if you cannot get
Expert in the respective artifact. A barbarian will do well with
Cleaver, but he may prefer a single handed weapon, an artifact long
sword or sabre, to have his hand free for a shield [of reflection],
or for two-weaponing.

> sword), even if the other weapon is superior, because if I do, I'll
> be missing 90% of the time, and it will take forever to gain any
> skill in two-handed sword to make it worth wielding!

With an unskilled weapon you really don't need much time to enhance
it to Basic. (It makes not much sense to try enhancing a restricted
weapon, though.)

> So that makes
> most of the weapons found in the dungeon absolutely useless to any
> character!

Yes, most of the weapons you find on the floor are just litter. But
you shouldn't try all things that you find lying around; focus on a
weapon or weapon-class that you intend to use throughout the game.
And get an artifact weapon as soon as possible.

